H('418.d.10.H',4,"Creating a 3D surface in CAD from survey points helps the designer see how the proposed grading changes the:","terrain",["weather","budget","zoning"],"Surface models show terrain.")
H('418.d.10.I',3,"After finishing a site design, the team shares it with reviewers and revises it based on their:","feedback",["lunch","jokes","uniforms"],"Presentation leads to improvement.")
H('418.d.10.I',4,"Presenting a site design clearly to reviewers requires labeling the key features and explaining how the design meets the:","project goals",["owner's hobbies","building colors only","bus schedule"],"Presentations connect design to goals.")
H('418.d.11.A',3,"A floor plan shows a room 12 ft by 15 ft. The area of its floor is:","180 square feet",["27 square feet","54 square feet","1,800 square feet"],"12 x 15 = 180.")
H('418.d.11.A',4,"Flooring comes in boxes covering 20 square feet. A 180 square foot room needs at least how many boxes?","9",["8","10","18"],"180 / 20 = 9.")
H('418.d.11.B',3,"A material quantity take-off for a wall counts the number of studs, sheets of drywall, and:","linear feet of trim",["clouds","songs","tenants"],"Take-offs list material amounts.")
H('418.d.11.B',4,"A wall is 40 ft long and 8 ft high, and studs are spaced 16 inches apart. The studs needed for the straight wall, counting one at the start, are about:","31",["10","41","25"],"480 inches / 16 = 30, plus one = 31.")
H('418.d.11.C',3,"An opinion of probable cost for a building adds the costs of materials, labor, equipment, and:","contractor overhead and profit",["lunch","paint choices","tour guides"],"An OPC includes all costs.")
H('418.d.11.C',4,"An OPC is prepared early in design to help the owner decide whether the project fits the available:","budget",["time","color","staff"],"Estimates guide go or no-go.")
H('418.d.11.D',3,"Documenting that a window meets the code requires the designer to cite the product's tested:","performance rating",["manufacturer's slogan","shipping color","shelf location"],"Documentation shows compliance.")
H('418.d.11.D',4,"The designer includes code notes on the drawings so that the inspector can verify that the building complies with the:","adopted code",["owner's wishes","crew's lunch","architect's mood"],"Notes prove compliance.")
H('418.d.11.E',3,"A bid tabulation lists the contractors' unit prices and totals side by side so the owner can compare:","bids",["menus","maps","songs"],"Tabs organize prices.")
H('418.d.11.E',4,"On a bid tabulation, the extended price for 200 square yards at 45 dollars per square yard is:","9,000 dollars",["245 dollars","900 dollars","4,500 dollars"],"200 x 45 = 9,000.")
H('418.d.11.F',3,"Checking that each pay item in a bid matches an item in the plans helps the engineer find a bidder who forgot to include the:","work",["lobby","address","phone"],"Complete bids cover all work.")
H('418.d.11.F',4,"If one bid's total is much lower than the rest, the engineer should check for missing items or unbalanced:","unit prices",["window styles","shipping dates","paint colors"],"Unusual bids need review.")
H('418.d.11.G',3,"To create a bid tabulation, the engineer lists every pay item in rows and each contractor's prices in:","columns",["poems","maps","songs"],"Tabs are tables.")
H('418.d.11.G',4,"The lowest total on a bid tabulation is not automatically the winner if the bidder is not:","responsible and responsive",["famous","local","well dressed"],"Awards require qualified bidders.")
H('418.d.11.H',3,"A construction project manual includes the bidding documents, the contract, and the written:","specifications",["cartoons","jokes","lyrics"],"The manual holds written requirements.")
H('418.d.11.H',4,"General conditions in a project manual describe the rights and duties of the owner and the:","contractor",["tourist","competitor","reporter"],"Conditions define roles.")
H('418.d.11.I',3,"An organizational chart for a construction project shows who reports to whom, such as the superintendent reporting to the:","project manager",["janitor","delivery driver","visitor"],"Org charts show reporting lines.")
H('418.d.11.I',4,"A Gantt chart for construction lists activities, such as foundation, framing, and roofing, in the order they must be:","performed",["named","painted","photographed"],"Sequences show order.")
H('419.d.1.A',3,"A county asks a survey firm why its road map keeps showing the wrong boundary. Stating this question precisely is the first step of:","defining the problem",["selling the maps","printing the maps","closing the office"],"Defining the problem starts the design process.")
H('419.d.1.A',4,"Skipping the problem-definition step often leads a surveying team to collect data that does not help answer the:","real question",["data sheet","budget form","weather report"],"A vague problem wastes field time.")
H('419.d.1.B',3,"Before staking a subdivision, a survey team studies old plats, deeds, and existing control points. This is the design process stage of:","research",["demolition","celebration","retirement"],"Research gathers background information.")
H('419.d.1.B',4,"Looking up how earlier surveyors marked corners helps a crew learn which methods are most likely to:","find the original corners",["lose the corners","move the corners","hide the corners"],"Past practice informs fieldwork.")
H('419.d.1.C',3,"Listing many possible ways to measure an inaccessible river width is an example of:","ideation",["inspection","closeout","billing"],"Ideation produces options.")
H('419.d.1.C',4,"Turning the river-width idea into a plan with equipment, steps, and expected accuracy is:","conceptualization",["abandonment","litigation","retirement"],"Concepts add detail to ideas.")
H('419.d.1.D',3,"A crew compares three ways to measure a long baseline and chooses the one with the best accuracy for the cost. This is:","selecting a solution",["naming a baseline","filing a plat","renewing a license"],"Selection compares options against criteria.")
H('419.d.1.D',4,"Choosing a method for a boundary survey should depend on the accuracy needed and the:","site conditions",["surveyor's hobbies","truck color","map border"],"Context drives method choice.")
H('419.d.1.E',3,"A hand-drawn sketch of a lot, a scaled map, and a 3D terrain model each serve a different purpose, but only the scaled map is a:","technical drawing",["cartoon","poem","greeting"],"Technical drawings follow standards.")
H('419.d.1.E',4,"A physical model of terrain built from survey data helps a client understand:","how the land slopes",["how much the surveyor charges","where the office is","what the weather was"],"Models communicate form.")
H('419.d.1.F',3,"A surveyor checks a new total station by measuring a distance already known to be exact. This kind of check is a:","calibration test",["social visit","parade","budget talk"],"Known references reveal error.")
H('419.d.1.F',4,"Repeating a measurement several times and comparing the results is part of designing a test that can show:","how reliable the method is",["how loud the crew is","how old the tape is","how far the van drove"],"Repetition measures reliability.")
H('419.d.1.G',3,"If field data show the traverse does not close, the crew goes back, rechecks the measurements, and tries again, which shows the process is:","iterative",["finished","guaranteed","accidental"],"Iteration refines results.")
H('419.d.1.G',4,"Reviewing a failed closure can reveal which step to improve, so feedback in an iterative process helps the team to:","improve the next attempt",["repeat the same error","skip the field work","hide the data"],"Feedback drives improvement.")
H('419.d.1.H',3,"After a survey is complete, the surveyor shares results with the client in a clear drawing and report. This is the purpose of:","communicating the solution",["destroying the data","hiding the map","skipping the report"],"Communication delivers the results.")
H('419.d.1.H',4,"A final survey plat must show enough detail that another surveyor could:","retrace the boundaries",["rename the roads","repaint the signs","redo the tax bills"],"Clear documentation supports repeatability.")
H('419.d.2.A',3,"A survey crew chief plans each day's field tasks around sunrise and traffic, practicing:","time management",["time travel","time zoning","time painting"],"Planning manages limited time.")
H('419.d.2.A',4,"Setting an internal deadline earlier than the client's helps a team handle surprises, such as a:","missing monument",["holiday party","new logo","phone upgrade"],"Buffers absorb delays.")
H('419.d.2.B',3,"A property that can only be surveyed after the owner gives permission to enter faces a constraint called:","legal access",["map color","weather","drawing scale"],"Access can limit work.")
H('419.d.2.B',4,"If heavy rain makes a river impassable, a crew plans the stream cross-section survey for another day, showing the value of:","planning around constraints",["ignoring weather","wasting time","avoiding work"],"Plans adapt to constraints.")
H('419.d.2.C',3,"Completing the control survey by Friday and the boundary drawing by next Wednesday are examples of:","milestones and deliverables",["jokes","hobbies","errors"],"Milestones mark progress.")
H('419.d.2.C',4,"Dividing a large survey into stages with deliverables lets the client review the work before it is:","finished",["named","lost","taxed"],"Stages support checkpoints.")
H('419.d.2.D',3,"A quick radio call between crew members is informal, while a certified plat delivered to a county is:","formal communication",["a rumor","small talk","a joke"],"Formal documents carry weight.")
H('419.d.2.D',4,"A surveyor emails a landowner a short question about a fence line. An email is a good choice because it creates a written:","record",["rumor","noise","guess"],"Written messages document facts.")
H('419.d.2.E',3,"When a measured angle does not agree with the plat, a crew rechecks each step one at a time, using a method of:","systematic troubleshooting",["wishful thinking","giving up","random guessing"],"Careful checking finds errors.")
H('419.d.2.E',4,"Working with other crew members to compare notes can solve a field problem faster because each person may notice a different:","detail",["rumor","hobby","holiday"],"Collaboration finds answers.")
H('419.d.3.A',3,"Employers must provide a worksite free of recognized serious hazards. Employees must also follow safety rules and use the:","required protective equipment",["fastest route","loudest radio","newest phone"],"Safety is a shared duty.")
H('419.d.3.A',4,"A surveyor who sees a hole hidden by tall grass near a traverse point should mark it and tell the crew, which shows the worker duty to:","report hazards",["avoid mapping","ignore the area","remove the notes"],"Workers share hazard information.")
H('419.d.3.B',3,"Standards from the Occupational Safety and Health Administration help protect workers by setting minimum rules for:","workplace safety",["lunch breaks only","map colors","tool prices"],"OSHA sets safety rules.")
H('419.d.3.B',4,"Road survey crews who work near traffic must follow OSHA rules, which are important because they reduce the chance of:","being struck by a vehicle",["running out of paint","losing a pencil","getting a sunburn"],"Work-zone safety saves lives.")
H('419.d.3.C',3,"Measuring noise or dust that crew members are exposed to on a job is part of industrial hygiene, which protects worker:","health",["fashion","pay","schedules"],"Hygiene prevents exposure illnesses.")
H('419.d.3.C',4,"Long-term sun exposure is a health hazard for field crews, and a control that helps is to use:","sunscreen, hats, and shade",["darker maps","longer tapes","bigger stakes"],"Controls reduce exposure.")
H('419.d.3.D',3,"A surveyor working along a highway wears a bright vest so drivers can see her, which is a type of:","personal protective equipment",["tracking device","calendar","drawing tool"],"High-visibility gear is PPE.")
H('419.d.3.D',4,"Steel-toe boots, gloves, and eye protection are chosen based on the hazards of the job, such as sharp tools and flying:","debris",["maps","songs","emails"],"PPE depends on the hazard.")
H('419.d.3.E',3,"Steep slopes, loose rocks, and uneven ground are examples of unsafe walking surfaces that can cause a:","slip or fall",["sunburn","sale","power outage"],"Footing matters outdoors.")
H('419.d.3.E',4,"When working on a slippery stream bank, a safe practice is to keep three points of contact and wear boots with good:","traction",["color","sound","paint"],"Traction prevents slips.")
H('419.d.3.F',3,"A survey rod made of metal can conduct electricity, so holding it near an overhead power line risks:","electrocution",["sunburn","boredom","paperwork"],"Metal rods near lines are deadly.")
H('419.d.3.F',4,"Before extending a long rod on a site, a crew member should look up to check for:","power lines",["birds","clouds","signs"],"Look for overhead hazards.")
H('419.d.3.G',3,"Fuel for a generator or paint cans for marking should be stored in approved containers because they can be:","flammable",["edible","colorful","musical"],"Fuels burn.")
H('419.d.3.G',4,"A crew that transports aerosol marking paint in a hot truck cab risks a can:","bursting from pressure",["changing color","gaining weight","freezing"],"Heat raises can pressure.")
H('419.d.3.H',3,"A first aid kit, a fire extinguisher, and a posted list of emergency phone numbers are workplace health and safety:","resources",["rumors","puzzles","games"],"Resources support response.")
H('419.d.3.H',4,"A crew working far from town should know the nearest hospital and carry a charged phone or radio so they can:","call for help",["order food","check scores","post photos"],"Emergency planning saves time.")
H('419.d.3.I',3,"A company safety and health program has management leadership, worker participation, and a process for finding and fixing:","hazards",["trophies","slogans","logos"],"Programs control hazards.")
H('419.d.3.I',4,"Asking field workers for input on a safety program improves it because they are closest to the real:","risks",["bills","maps","emails"],"Worker insight improves programs.")
H('419.d.3.J',3,"A written emergency action plan tells workers how to report a fire and how to:","evacuate",["order lunch","change shifts","buy tools"],"Plans give clear steps.")
H('419.d.3.J',4,"Doing a drill of the plan helps workers find weak spots in the procedure before a real:","emergency",["vacation","holiday","delivery"],"Drills reveal gaps.")
H('419.d.3.K',3,"The hazard communication standard requires employers to train workers about the chemicals they use and to keep:","safety data sheets",["lunch menus","vacation lists","sale flyers"],"HazCom requires SDSs.")
H('419.d.3.K',4,"A label with an exclamation mark pictogram warns of a less serious hazard, such as skin:","irritation",["decoration","music","height"],"Pictograms show hazard types.")
H('419.d.3.L',3,"New employees must be trained on the hazards of their tasks before they:","begin the work",["get a raise","go home","sign a lease"],"Training comes first.")
H('419.d.3.L',4,"Annual refresher training helps workers stay current on safety rules, which are updated as:","hazards and regulations change",["menus rotate","holidays pass","seasons start"],"Retraining keeps skills current.")
H('419.d.4.A',3,"Distances measured as 100.2, 100.4, and 100.0 meters have a mean of:","100.2 m",["100.4 m","300.6 m","100.0 m"],"(100.2 + 100.4 + 100.0)/3 = 100.2.")
H('419.d.4.A',4,"Elevations of 10.1, 10.3, 10.2, 10.2, and 11.0 feet have a median of:","10.2 ft",["10.3 ft","10.56 ft","11.0 ft"],"The middle value of the ordered data is 10.2.")
H('419.d.4.B',3,"A set of angle readings that are all very close together has a small:","standard deviation",["mean angle","maximum angle","sum of angles"],"Tight data have low spread.")
H('419.d.4.B',4,"If two crews measure the same line and Crew A's readings spread more widely than Crew B's, Crew A's standard deviation is:","larger",["smaller","zero","negative"],"More spread, larger SD.")
H('419.d.4.C',3,"The bell-shaped curve that describes many random measurement errors is called a:","normal distribution",["square wave","straight line","spiral"],"Random errors are often normally distributed.")
H('419.d.4.C',4,"The peak of the normal curve of a set of repeated distance measurements is centered at the:","mean",["maximum reading","minimum reading","range"],"The center is the average.")
H('419.d.4.D',3,"The Empirical Rule predicts that about 95 percent of data lie within two standard deviations of the:","mean",["median only","maximum","range"],"68-95-99.7.")
H('419.d.4.D',4,"A measurement set has mean 50.00 m and standard deviation 0.02 m. About 68 percent of values will lie between:","49.98 m and 50.02 m",["49.96 m and 50.04 m","49.90 m and 50.10 m","49.00 m and 51.00 m"],"Mean plus or minus 1 SD.")
H('419.d.4.E',3,"A tape that is stretched slightly wrong causes every measurement to be long by the same amount. This is a:","systematic error",["random error","blunder","tolerance"],"Constant bias is systematic.")
H('419.d.4.E',4,"Small, unpredictable variations from reading to reading, caused by things like wind or hand tremor, are called:","random errors",["systematic errors","blunders","corrections"],"Random errors vary unpredictably.")
H('419.d.4.F',3,"Shots that are tightly clustered but far from the bullseye describe measurements that are:","precise but not accurate",["accurate but not precise","both accurate and precise","neither precise nor accurate"],"Tight cluster off target.")
H('419.d.4.F',4,"Shots scattered around the bullseye with the average at the center describe measurements that are:","accurate on average but not precise",["precise and inaccurate","useless always","exactly perfect"],"Spread but centered.")
H('419.d.4.G',3,"A polygon with five sides is a:","pentagon",["hexagon","octagon","triangle"],"Pentagon means five sides.")
H('419.d.4.G',4,"The sum of the interior angles of any four-sided polygon is:","360 degrees",["180 degrees","540 degrees","720 degrees"],"A quadrilateral sums to 360.")
H('419.d.4.H',3,"A right triangle has legs 6 ft and 8 ft. By the Pythagorean theorem the hypotenuse is:","10 ft",["14 ft","100 ft","48 ft"],"36 + 64 = 100, square root = 10.")
H('419.d.4.H',4,"From a point 60 ft from the base of a 60 ft tower on level ground, the angle of elevation to the top is:","45 degrees",["30 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees"],"tan 45 = 1, so distance equals height.")
H('419.d.4.I',3,"A line segment connecting two points on a circle and passing through its center is a:","diameter",["tangent","arc","sector"],"A diameter passes through the center.")
H('419.d.4.I',4,"A segment that touches a circle at one point and does not cross inside is a:","tangent",["chord","radius","secant"],"Tangents touch once.")
H('419.d.4.J',3,"A circular tank has a diameter of 10 m. Its radius is:","5 m",["20 m","10 m","2.5 m"],"Radius is half the diameter.")
H('419.d.4.J',4,"A circle has a radius of 3 m. Using pi = 3.14, its area is about:","28.3 square meters",["18.8 square meters","9.4 square meters","56.5 square meters"],"3.14 x 9 = 28.26.")
H('419.d.4.K',3,"A road rises 2 feet for every 50 feet of horizontal distance. Its slope as a fraction is:","1/25",["25","1/2","50"],"2/50 = 1/25.")
H('419.d.4.K',4,"A line has the equation y = 3x - 2. Its y-intercept is:","-2",["3","2","-3"],"In y = mx + b, b is the y-intercept.")
H('419.d.4.L',3,"The volume of a rectangular box 4 m by 3 m by 2 m is:","24 cubic meters",["9 cubic meters","12 cubic meters","48 cubic meters"],"4 x 3 x 2 = 24.")
H('419.d.4.L',4,"A cylinder-shaped well casing has a radius of 0.5 ft and a height of 20 ft. Using pi = 3.14, the volume is about:","15.7 cubic feet",["31.4 cubic feet","3.14 cubic feet","62.8 cubic feet"],"3.14 x 0.25 x 20 = 15.7.")
H('419.d.5.A',3,"Imaginary lines that run east-west and measure position north or south of the equator are lines of:","latitude",["longitude","altitude","magnitude"],"Latitude measures north-south position.")
H('419.d.5.A',4,"Longitude measures position east or west of the:","prime meridian",["equator","tropic","pole"],"Prime meridian is 0 degrees longitude.")
H('419.d.5.B',3,"A GPS survey method that uses a base station and a rover, and gets centimeter accuracy, is called:","real-time kinematic",["static camera","dead reckoning","radio guess"],"RTK uses corrections.")
H('419.d.5.B',4,"Static GPS surveying collects data over a longer time than RTK and is used when the highest possible:","accuracy is required",["speed is needed","cost is irrelevant","price is low"],"Long sessions improve accuracy.")
H('419.d.5.C',3,"Occupying a mark with a GPS receiver for several hours and processing the data to get its latitude and longitude is called tying down a:","point",["road","contract","bill"],"Tie-downs establish coordinates.")
H('419.d.5.C',4,"A tie-down to the National Spatial Reference System gives the point coordinates that match the national:","datum",["calendar","tax roll","clock"],"Coordinates reference a datum.")
H('419.d.5.D',3,"GPS has three segments: the space segment of satellites, the control segment of ground stations, and the user segment of:","receivers",["maps","tapes","pencils"],"Receivers are the user segment.")
H('419.d.5.D',4,"The control segment of GPS monitors the satellites and updates their:","orbit and clock data",["paint","color","weight"],"Ground stations maintain satellite data.")
H('419.d.5.E',3,"A GPS satellite continuously broadcasts its position and the exact:","time",["weather","price","color"],"Timing signals enable ranging.")
H('419.d.5.E',4,"GPS satellites carry atomic clocks because positioning depends on measuring tiny differences in signal:","travel time",["color","sound","volume"],"Timing errors become range errors.")
H('419.d.5.F',3,"GPS ground stations track the satellites and send them corrections so that their signals remain:","accurate",["colorful","loud","slow"],"Control stations correct orbits.")
H('419.d.5.F',4,"The master control station for GPS collects data from monitor stations around the world to calculate precise:","satellite orbits",["road maps","tax rates","time zones"],"Orbits are computed from monitor data.")
H('419.d.5.G',3,"A GPS receiver measures its distance from several satellites to determine its:","position",["weight","age","color"],"Receivers compute location.")
H('419.d.5.G',4,"A receiver needs signals from at least four satellites to solve for its three position coordinates and its:","clock error",["tire pressure","battery size","paint color"],"Four unknowns need four signals.")
H('419.d.5.H',3,"A map made using geodetic coordinates plots points by their latitude and longitude on a model of the:","Earth",["moon","sun","office"],"Geodetic coordinates reference the Earth.")
H('419.d.5.H',4,"When plotting survey points on a flat map from geodetic coordinates, the surveyor first converts them with a map:","projection",["cartoon","legend","border"],"Projections flatten the globe.")
H('419.d.6.A',3,"A theodolite combines a telescope with graduated circles that measure horizontal and vertical:","angles",["weights","colors","prices"],"Optomechanical instruments measure angles.")
H('419.d.6.A',4,"The leveling screws and bubble on a survey instrument are used to make its vertical axis truly:","plumb",["colorful","hot","portable"],"Leveling aligns the instrument.")
H('419.d.6.B',3,"An instrument that sets a horizontal line of sight so that rod readings can find differences in height is the:","level",["compass rose","plumb bob only","drafting pencil"],"Levels measure elevation differences.")
H('419.d.6.B',4,"A total station combines an angle-measuring theodolite with an electronic way to measure:","distance",["weather","color","sound"],"Total stations measure angles and distances.")
H('419.d.6.C',3,"A small aircraft flown without an onboard pilot and used to photograph a site for mapping is a:","UAV or drone",["bulldozer","tape","stake"],"UAVs collect aerial data.")
H('419.d.6.C',4,"A remote sensing method that sends laser pulses to the ground to measure many elevation points rapidly is:","LiDAR",["radio guess","paper tracing","hand sketch"],"LiDAR creates dense point clouds.")
H('419.d.6.D',3,"A steel tape, an odometer wheel, and an electronic range finder are all tools for measuring:","distance",["time","temperature","angles"],"These tools measure length.")
H('419.d.6.D',4,"Over a long line, the most accurate of a steel tape, a measuring wheel, and an instrument that times a reflected signal is typically the:","electronic distance meter",["measuring wheel","paced steps","guess"],"EDMs are precise over long ranges.")
H('419.d.6.E',3,"Measuring a line by laying a tape end to end along the ground is called:","taping",["pacing","sighting","plotting"],"Taping is direct measurement.")
H('419.d.6.E',4,"Methods that determine distance by measuring the time a signal takes to travel to a target and back are called:","electronic distance measurement",["pacing","estimating","chaining by eye"],"EDM measures travel time.")
H('419.d.6.F',3,"A crew measures the distance between two points on the ground twice, once with a tape and once with an EDM. The first step in doing this is to:","set up and record each reading",["destroy the stakes","ignore the readings","guess"],"Careful recording supports comparison.")
H('419.d.6.F',4,"When measuring a slope distance with a tape, the surveyor must correct the reading to a:","horizontal distance",["vertical angle only","color","lower number"],"Surveys use horizontal distances.")
H('419.d.6.G',3,"A tape reading of 100.03 m and an EDM reading of 100.01 m differ by:","0.02 m",["2 m","0.2 m","200 m"],"100.03 - 100.01 = 0.02.")
H('419.d.6.G',4,"After comparing the data from two measuring tools, the crew should trust the result from the tool that has the smaller:","uncertainty",["weight","price","handle"],"Smaller uncertainty is better.")
H('419.d.6.H',3,"A protractor, a compass, and a theodolite are tools for measuring:","angles",["distances","weights","times"],"These measure angular data.")
H('419.d.6.H',4,"Of a protractor, a compass, and an instrument with a telescope and graduated circles, the one with the finest angular readout is the:","theodolite",["compass","protractor","ruler"],"Theodolites read to seconds.")
H('419.d.6.I',3,"Measuring the angle between two lines of sight by turning a telescope from one target to the other uses the method of:","direct angle measurement",["pacing","estimating","tracing"],"Angles are read between sightings.")
H('419.d.6.I',4,"Repeating an angle several times and averaging the results is a way to reduce the effect of:","random reading errors",["gravity","weather forecasts","sunlight"],"Repetition reduces random error.")
H('419.d.6.J',3,"A magnetic compass shows an angle called a bearing, which is measured from:","north",["the equator","the nearest road","the sun at noon"],"Bearings reference north.")
H('419.d.6.J',4,"The difference between true north and magnetic north at a location is called:","magnetic declination",["scale factor","contour interval","traverse error"],"Compasses need correction for declination.")
H('419.d.6.K',3,"A theodolite reads an angle as 45 degrees 10 minutes, and a compass gives 46 degrees. The two differ by:","about 50 minutes",["about 5 minutes","about 5 degrees","about 90 degrees"],"46° = 46°00′; difference = 50′.")
H('419.d.6.K',4,"When different angle-measuring tools disagree, the surveyor should consider the precision of each tool and check for:","blunders or setup errors",["extra data","tool color","weather names"],"Disagreement signals errors.")
H('419.d.6.L',3,"The National Geodetic Survey maintains a network of points with known positions called:","control points",["markers of luck","price points","street signs"],"Control points anchor surveys.")
H('419.d.6.L',4,"A surveyor ties a new survey to NGS monuments so that the survey's coordinates agree with the:","national reference system",["local newspaper","owner's guess","tax roll"],"Ties unify surveys.")
H('419.d.6.M',3,"A level, a theodolite, and a GPS receiver can each be used to determine a point's:","elevation",["taste","weight","price"],"These tools can measure height.")
H('419.d.6.M',4,"Of those tools, the level is generally most precise for finding the:","difference in height between nearby points",["position on the globe","angle to the sun","size of a lot"],"Differential leveling is precise.")
H('419.d.6.N',3,"A theodolite reads a vertical angle of 45 degrees to the top of a tower. The tower's height above the instrument divided by the horizontal distance to it equals the tangent of 45 degrees, which is:","1",["0","0.5","2"],"tan 45 = 1.")
H('419.d.6.N',4,"To find the full height of an object using a theodolite, the surveyor adds the calculated rise to the:","height of the instrument",["owner's name","map scale","zip code"],"The telescope is above the ground.")
H('419.d.6.O',3,"A benchmark has an elevation of 100.00 ft. A rod reading on the benchmark is 4.00 ft, and a rod reading on a new point is 6.50 ft. The new point is:","2.50 ft lower",["2.50 ft higher","10.50 ft higher","4.00 ft higher"],"100.00 + 4.00 - 6.50 = 97.50.")
H('419.d.6.O',4,"Starting from a benchmark of known elevation, a leveling run determines the elevations of other points using the principle of:","height of instrument",["time of flight","magnetic bearing","latitude"],"HI = elevation + backsight.")
H('419.d.6.P',3,"Two ways of finding the same elevation give 612.31 ft and 612.28 ft. The difference between them is:","0.03 ft",["0.3 ft","3.0 ft","0.003 ft"],"612.31 - 612.28 = 0.03.")
H('419.d.6.P',4,"When elevation values from two methods differ by more than the tolerance, the crew should:","recheck the work",["average them blindly","ignore both","erase the notes"],"Large differences signal a problem.")
H('419.d.6.Q',3,"Federal Aviation Administration rules require a person who flies a drone for commercial survey work to hold a:","remote pilot certificate",["fishing license","boat permit","parking pass"],"Part 107 requires certification.")
H('419.d.6.Q',4,"A UAV operator must keep the aircraft within visual line of sight and below a maximum altitude of:","400 feet above ground level",["4,000 feet","40 feet","40,000 feet"],"Part 107 sets limits.")
H('419.d.6.R',3,"A survey that shows the location of existing features and ground heights, used to design a project, is a:","topographic survey",["hydrographic survey only","tax survey","census survey"],"Topographic surveys map terrain.")
H('419.d.6.R',4,"A survey that maps the depth of the bottom of a lake or river is called a:","hydrographic survey",["boundary survey","construction survey","topographic survey"],"Hydrographic surveys map underwater.")
H('419.d.7.A',3,"A surveyor records measurements as they are taken in a bound field book so there is a permanent record of the:","fieldwork",["lunch","weather alone","music"],"Field notes document the work.")
H('419.d.7.A',4,"An error in a field book should be crossed out with a single line, not erased, so the original remains:","legible",["hidden","destroyed","missing"],"Never erase field notes.")
H('419.d.7.B',3,"The cover page of a field book includes the owner's name, the book number, and the dates:","covered",["spoken","hidden","lost"],"Cover pages identify the book.")
H('419.d.7.B',4,"A table of contents in a field book lets a later reader find a particular survey by its page and:","date",["color","weight","price"],"Contents aid retrieval.")
H('419.d.7.C',3,"A level run recorded in a field book lists the station, backsight, height of instrument, foresight, and:","elevation",["lunch","color","name"],"Level notes record each step.")
H('419.d.7.C',4,"Recording the weather, instrument serial number, and crew members in a field book helps a later reviewer judge the:","conditions and reliability of the data",["color of the tape","price of the survey","name of the road"],"Metadata supports review.")
H('419.d.8.A',3,"A control survey establishes a framework of precisely located points to which later surveys can be:","tied",["sold","erased","painted"],"Control provides a reference.")
H('419.d.8.A',4,"A control survey begins with a reconnaissance of the site and then the setting of monuments, followed by measurements and the:","adjustment of the data",["sale of the land","closing of the office","printing of the tax bill"],"Adjustment distributes errors.")
H('419.d.8.B',3,"A map legend explains the meaning of the symbols on a survey drawing, such as one for a fire hydrant or a:","utility pole",["cartoon","song","tax form"],"Symbols represent features.")
H('419.d.8.B',4,"A north arrow on a survey drawing is needed so the reader can tell the drawing's:","orientation",["owner","price","weather"],"A north arrow gives direction.")
H('419.d.8.C',3,"Software that lets a surveyor draw maps and compute coordinates from field data is called:","CAD or survey software",["a game","a browser only","a calculator app only"],"Survey software supports mapping.")
H('419.d.8.C',4,"Survey software can check whether a traverse closes by computing the closure:","error",["paint","song","tax"],"Traverse closure shows quality.")
H('419.d.9.A',3,"Drafting a construction document to scale means each dimension on the sheet is a fixed fraction of the real:","length",["color","weight","time"],"Scale preserves proportion.")
H('419.d.9.A',4,"At a scale of 1 inch = 50 feet, a feature 200 feet long measures how many inches on the sheet?","4",["2","8","10"],"200 / 50 = 4.")
H('419.d.9.B',3,"To fit a long site onto a small sheet, the drafter chooses a smaller scale, such as:","1 inch = 100 feet",["1 inch = 1 foot","1 inch = 5 feet","1 inch = 10 feet"],"Larger ratio means smaller drawing.")
H('419.d.9.B',4,"A site is 600 feet wide and the sheet has room for 12 inches. The largest convenient scale that fits is:","1 inch = 50 feet",["1 inch = 20 feet","1 inch = 10 feet","1 inch = 5 feet"],"600 / 12 = 50.")
H('419.d.9.C',3,"If the written specifications and the drawing disagree on a pipe size, the drafter and engineer must resolve the:","conflict",["color","title","date"],"Specs and drawings must agree.")
H('419.d.9.C',4,"A drawing shows what is to be built and where, while the specification states the:","quality and method of work",["colors of the roads","names of the crew","costs of lunch"],"Specs describe materials and workmanship.")
H('419.d.9.D',3,"The distance between two points on a map projection plane is called the grid distance, while the distance on the ground at the surface is the:","ground distance",["bearing distance","paper distance","angle distance"],"Grid and ground distances differ.")
H('419.d.9.D',4,"Grid and surface distances differ because the Earth's surface is curved and the projection flattens it, causing a small:","distortion",["rainstorm","delay","bonus"],"Projections introduce distortion.")
H('419.d.9.E',3,"A combined scale factor is multiplied by a ground distance to convert it to a:","grid distance",["time","weight","bearing"],"Scale factors transform distances.")
H('419.d.9.E',4,"A ground distance of 1,000.00 ft with a combined scale factor of 0.9999 gives a grid distance of:","999.90 ft",["1,000.10 ft","1,001.00 ft","990.00 ft"],"1000 x 0.9999 = 999.9.")
H('419.d.9.F',3,"A topographic map made from field data shows the shape of the land using lines of equal elevation called:","contours",["borders","roads","hedges"],"Contours show relief.")
H('419.d.9.F',4,"Closely spaced contour lines on a topographic map indicate that the land is:","steep",["flat","underwater","level"],"Close lines mean steep ground.")
H('419.d.9.G',3,"A surface profile drawn along a baseline on a topographic map shows ground elevation versus:","distance along the line",["time of day","color of soil","owner name"],"Profiles plot elevation against distance.")
H('419.d.9.G',4,"A profile of a proposed road is drawn with the vertical scale exaggerated compared with the horizontal scale to make changes in:","elevation easier to see",["distance easier to hide","color easier to match","labels easier to read"],"Vertical exaggeration clarifies relief.")
H('419.d.9.H',3,"Placing stakes in the field at the locations shown on a design plan is called:","staking out",["staking in","sketching out","clearing in"],"Stakeout transfers a design to the ground.")
H('419.d.9.H',4,"To stake a point from design coordinates, a surveyor uses a total station to turn the proper angle and measure the proper:","distance",["color","song","weight"],"Stakeout needs angle and distance.")
H('419.d.10.A',3,"When a surveyor finds that a previous surveyor set a monument and the neighbors have relied on it for years, she usually must defer to the work of the:","original survey",["newest guess","tax assessor","neighbor's opinion"],"Surveyors follow original evidence.")
H('419.d.10.A',4,"Surveyors retrace original surveys rather than redefine property lines because landowners rely on the boundaries that were:","originally established",["newly invented","randomly chosen","privately changed"],"Retracement protects stability.")
H('419.d.10.B',3,"Placing permanent markers at the corners of a parcel is called boundary:","monumentation",["demolition","advertising","relocation"],"Monuments mark corners.")
H('419.d.10.B',4,"A monument is useful as evidence of a boundary only if it is permanent, identifiable, and can be:","found later",["moved easily","hidden","painted over"],"Evidence must be recoverable.")
H('419.d.10.C',3,"A large oak tree or a river bank used to mark a property corner is a natural:","monument",["map symbol","tax stamp","distance"],"Natural features mark corners.")
H('419.d.10.C',4,"An iron rod driven in the ground or a concrete marker is an example of an:","artificial monument",["natural monument","error","plat"],"Human-made markers are artificial monuments.")
H('419.d.10.D',3,"When a river slowly deposits soil on one bank, the boundary of the land may shift according to the rule of:","accretion",["avulsion","reliction","partition"],"Gradual change adjusts the boundary.")
H('419.d.10.D',4,"A sudden change in a river's course during a flood is called avulsion, and it usually leaves the boundary:","unchanged at the old location",["moved to the new channel","erased","given away"],"Sudden changes do not shift boundaries.")
H('419.d.10.E',3,"A legal description of a property uses words, bearings, and distances, or a lot and block reference, to identify the parcel's:","exact location",["history of owners","assessed taxes","paint color"],"Legal descriptions locate land.")
H('419.d.10.E',4,"The phrase \"Lot 5, Block 3 of the Oak Hills Addition\" is a legal description based on a recorded:","plat",["recipe","poem","photo"],"Lot and block refer to a plat.")
H('419.d.10.F',3,"To find where an original survey placed a boundary, a surveyor researches deeds, plats, and other land:","records",["menus","tickets","trophies"],"Records show the original survey.")
H('419.d.10.F',4,"Reading the field notes of the first surveyor can help locate a missing corner by describing nearby natural and artificial:","objects",["quotes","grades","prices"],"Original notes include bearing trees and objects.")
H('419.d.10.G',3,"Much of the land in Texas was first granted to settlers by Spain, Mexico, and later the:","Republic of Texas",["Roman Empire","British Crown","Ottoman Empire"],"Historic land grants shape titles.")
H('419.d.10.G',4,"Understanding Texas land grants is important to surveyors because many current property boundaries trace back to the:","original grant surveys",["recent fads","modern roads","mapping apps"],"Titles descend from grants.")
H('419.d.10.H',3,"A boundary survey protects the public by helping to prevent disputes and by showing the extent of a landowner's:","property rights",["favorite colors","taxes owed to a friend","television rights"],"Surveys clarify ownership.")
H('419.d.10.H',4,"A boundary survey can also protect a buyer from discovering after closing that a neighbor's building:","encroaches on the lot",["is painted blue","has a name","is visible"],"Surveys reveal encroachments.")
H('419.d.10.I',3,"Using collected field data, a surveyor draws a boundary map that shows the property's corners, bearings, and:","distances",["colors","songs","slogans"],"Boundary drawings show lengths and directions.")
H('419.d.10.I',4,"A boundary drawing includes a title block, a north arrow, a scale, and the surveyor's seal and:","signature",["pet's name","lunch menu","bank number"],"Seals and signatures certify the plat.")
H('419.d.10.J',3,"In the order of dignity of calls, natural objects like a creek rank above:","artificial monuments",["no other call","nothing","every other call"],"Natural objects are highest.")
H('419.d.10.J',4,"If a deed gives a distance of 200 feet but an iron rod is found 203 feet away at the described corner, the dignity of calls says to trust the:","monument",["distance","area","number"],"Monuments outrank distances.")
H('419.d.11.A',3,"One international foot equals exactly:","0.3048 meter",["0.3937 meter","3.2808 meters","1.0000 meter"],"Defined as 0.3048 m.")
H('419.d.11.A',4,"A rod 3.048 meters long equals how many international feet?","10",["3","30","100"],"3.048 / 0.3048 = 10.")
H('419.d.11.B',3,"A right angle measures:","90 degrees",["45 degrees","180 degrees","360 degrees"],"Right angles are 90 degrees.")
H('419.d.11.B',4,"One degree contains how many minutes of arc?","60",["10","100","360"],"1 degree = 60 minutes.")
H('419.d.11.C',3,"Excavation volume is often reported in cubic:","yards",["pounds","hours","minutes"],"Yards measure earthwork volume.")
H('419.d.11.C',4,"One cubic yard equals how many cubic feet?","27",["9","3","81"],"3 x 3 x 3 = 27.")
H('419.d.11.D',3,"One acre equals:","43,560 square feet",["4,356 square feet","435,600 square feet","4,000 square feet"],"Standard definition.")
H('419.d.11.D',4,"A hectare is a metric unit of area equal to how many square meters?","10,000",["1,000","100","100,000"],"1 ha = 100 m x 100 m.")
H('419.d.11.E',3,"To change 2 acres to square feet, multiply 2 by:","43,560",["4,356","3,560","5,280"],"2 x 43,560 = 87,120.")
H('419.d.11.E',4,"A tract measures 87,120 square feet. Its area in acres is:","2",["1","3","4"],"87,120 / 43,560 = 2.")
H('419.d.11.F',3,"A level run starts at a benchmark and ends 15 inches higher than where it began. In feet, the change in elevation is:","+1.25 ft",["-1.25 ft","+12.5 ft","0 ft"],"Change is the final minus the starting.")
H('419.d.11.F',4,"A surveyor reads backsight 5.20 ft and foresight 3.10 ft. The new point is higher than the old by:","2.10 ft",["8.30 ft","5.20 ft","3.10 ft"],"BS - FS = 2.10.")
H('419.d.11.G',3,"An industry-accepted method for measuring the horizontal distance between two points far apart over open ground is:","electronic distance measurement",["pacing only","eyeballing","string by hand"],"EDM is accurate over distance.")
H('419.d.11.G',4,"Surveyors accept GPS and EDM for long distances because both provide the:","required accuracy",["lowest cost always","fastest sound","smallest instrument"],"Accuracy requirements decide the method.")
H('419.d.11.H',3,"A tape reading differs from the EDM reading by 0.04 ft. The error expressed relative to a 400 ft line is:","1 in 10,000",["1 in 100","1 in 1,000","1 in 40"],"0.04 / 400 = 1/10,000.")
H('419.d.11.H',4,"A method whose error is 1 in 5,000 is less precise than one with an error of:","1 in 20,000",["1 in 1,000","1 in 500","1 in 100"],"Larger denominator is better precision.")
H('419.d.11.I',3,"Errors in survey data are classified as blunders, systematic errors, and:","random errors",["colored errors","mapped errors","taxed errors"],"Three classes of error.")
H('419.d.11.I',4,"A traverse angle is recorded as 62 degrees when the correct value is 26 degrees because the digits were swapped. This is a:","blunder",["random error","systematic error","tolerance"],"A transposition is a blunder.")
H('419.d.12.A',3,"Eratosthenes estimated the circumference of the Earth by comparing the lengths of shadows at two different:","cities",["moons","seasons","lakes"],"He used Syene and Alexandria.")
H('419.d.12.A',4,"Eratosthenes found that the angle of the shadow difference was about one fiftieth of a circle, so the Earth's circumference was about 50 times the distance between:","the two cities",["two stars","two oceans","two moons"],"Proportion gave the size.")
H('419.d.12.B',3,"Surveyors once measured the Earth with chains and sextants, while modern surveyors use satellites, which has greatly improved:","precision",["weight","price of land","seasons"],"Methods improved over time.")
H('419.d.12.B',4,"The development of the theodolite, then the electronic distance meter, then GPS each increased the speed and accuracy of measuring the:","size and shape of the Earth",["number of surveyors","price of maps","names of states"],"Technology advanced geodesy.")
H('419.d.12.C',3,"Surveyors laid out the line and grade for large works such as canals, railroads, and:","dams",["songs","menus","stories"],"Surveying supports big projects.")
H('419.d.12.C',4,"The Panama Canal depended on accurate surveying to control the water levels and the locations of its:","locks",["teams","colors","maps"],"Elevations determined lock design.")
H('419.d.13.A',3,"Surveyors use legal definitions such as \"easement,\" \"encroachment,\" and \"boundary\" to describe the rights tied to:","real property",["music","movies","fashion"],"Legal terms define land rights.")
H('419.d.13.A',4,"The legal term for a structure that extends across a property line onto a neighbor's land is an:","encroachment",["easement","monument","covenant"],"Encroachments cross boundaries.")
H('419.d.13.B',3,"The Texas rules for professional land surveyors require them to hold paramount the public's:","safety and welfare",["comfort","entertainment","opinion"],"Safety comes first.")
H('419.d.13.B',4,"A licensed surveyor asked by a client to alter a boundary measurement to favor the client should:","refuse and report accurately",["agree quietly","hide the field notes","charge more"],"Honesty is required.")

H('418.d.9.L',4,"A drawing of the front face of a house, flattened without perspective, is called an:","elevation",["plan","roof detail","site survey"],"Elevations show the vertical faces of a building.")

B.balance()
B.save(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), '..', 'batches', 'cte_68.jsonl'))
